.TH ORBITAL 2
.SH NAME
orbital: psi, radial, angular, peak, extent \- hydrogen wave functions
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
include "orbital.m";
orb := load Orbital Orbital->PATH;
orb->init();

v   := orb->psi(n, l, m, z, x, y, zc);
r   := orb->radial(n, l, z, r);
a   := orb->angular(l, m, x, y, zc);
pk  := orb->peak(n, l, m, z, half, steps);
h   := orb->extent(n, z);
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Psi
is psi_nlm at a point: R_nl(r) times the REAL spherical harmonic, in
atomic units, for nuclear charge
.IR z .
It returns 0 for any combination that is not a state \(em l outside
0..n\-1, or |m| above l \(em rather than an approximation to one.
.PP
The angular part is the real form, the combinations of +m and \-m with
names: p_x, p_y, p_z, d_z2 and the rest.  The complex Y_lm have constant
modulus in phi and would draw as featureless rings; these are what has a
shape, and what the sign of a lobe means anything for.
.PP
.B Angular
takes a direction as a VECTOR and not as angles, because a caller
walking a grid has one and would otherwise compute an arctangent it does
not want.  cos(m phi) and sin(m phi) come from a recurrence on
(x + iy)^m, so nothing goes wrong on the axis.
.PP
Nothing is written out per state.  The radial part is the general
formula with an associated Laguerre polynomial by recurrence and the
angular part an associated Legendre, so n is limited only by the
factorials rather than by whoever's patience ran out writing cases.
.SS Phase
.B Legendre
carries the Condon-Shortley phase \(em P_1^1 is MINUS sin(theta) \(em
and
.B angular
takes it back out again with a factor of (\-1)^m, which is the usual
convention for the real harmonics and the one that puts p_x's positive
lobe at positive x.
.PP
Which lobe is positive is a convention and not physics: psi and \-psi
are the same state.  But it is a convention a PICTURE shows, so
.IR orbitaltest (1)
pins it rather than leaving it free to flip.
.SS Scaling a picture
.B Extent
is a sensible half-width to draw a state in.  Orbitals grow as n^2/z, so
one fixed box is wrong for every n but one.
.B Peak
is the largest |psi| on a grid of that half-width, which is what a
picture needs to scale itself and what a sampler needs for a threshold.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/lib/orbital.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR danby-orbital (1),
.IR orbitaltest (1),
.IR danby-hydrogen (1)
.SH BUGS
.B Peak
walks a cube and costs steps^3 evaluations.  It is meant to be called
when something changes, not per frame.
.PP
There is no spin, so nothing here has four quantum numbers, and no
electron-electron anything: this is hydrogen, or a one-electron ion, and
not an atom with a filled shell.
